<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Aristonicus</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">d. c. 128 BCE</span> · <a href="/centuries/2nd-century-bce">2nd century BCE</a> · Also known as Eumenes III</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Aristonicus was a pretender to the throne of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pergamon">Pergamon</a> who claimed to be a son of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eumenes-ii">Eumenes II</a> and took the royal name Eumenes III. When <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/attalus-iii">Attalus III</a> died in 133 BCE leaving his kingdom to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a>, Aristonicus led a revolt that drew support from slaves and the poor of western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anatolia">Anatolia</a>. He was captured by the consul <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/marcus-perperna-vento">Marcus Perperna</a> in 130 BCE and later executed in Rome, and the kingdom became the province of Asia.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/aristonicus.jpg" alt="Aristonicus" width="320" height="454" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Maurice Flesier · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Statue_of_Eumenes_III_(cropped).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
